  'content'=><<<'HTML'
<p><strong>To use Freshdesk well, set up the support channel first, then build the ticket workflow around your real queues.</strong> Confirm whether you need standard email-first Freshdesk or Freshdesk Omni, connect the support mailbox, organize agents and groups, and only then enable automation and service-level targets. The steps below follow Freshworks help documentation checked October 28, 2026.</p>
<div class="software-detail__callout"><p><strong>Before you start:</strong> The standard Freshdesk product is email-first. Freshdesk Omni supports a broader channel mix. If your team relies on WhatsApp, Instagram or advanced web chat, check the current <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000011785-getting-started-with-freshdesk-omni">official product comparison</a> before following a setup guide.</p></div>
<nav class="software-detail__toc" aria-label="On this page"><h2>On this page</h2><ol><li><a href="#requirements">What you need first</a></li><li><a href="#step-1">Step 1: Choose the product</a></li><li><a href="#step-2">Step 2: Connect support email</a></li><li><a href="#step-3">Step 3: Organize agents and tickets</a></li><li><a href="#step-4">Step 4: Add automation rules</a></li><li><a href="#step-5">Step 5: Set SLA policies</a></li><li><a href="#step-6">Step 6: Test the customer journey</a></li><li><a href="#knowledge">Create a useful knowledge base</a></li><li><a href="#exports">Export records safely</a></li><li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li></ol></nav>
<h2 id="requirements">What you need first</h2>
<p>Use an account with the correct administrator permissions. Have access to the support email domain, know which people will own each type of ticket, and agree on the response targets your team can meet. If you are moving from another platform, list the records and rules you need to keep before you configure the new help desk.</p>
<ul><li>One verified support address and access to its forwarding or mail settings.</li><li>A list of agent groups such as billing, technical support or returns.</li><li>Ticket fields and tags that help route work without making forms hard to complete.</li><li>Clear response and resolution targets by priority or request type.</li><li>A short test plan covering email receipt, assignment, reply, closure and customer notification.</li></ul>
<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Choose the correct Freshdesk product</h2>
<p>Freshworks documents two current products with different channel coverage. Standard Freshdesk is designed for email-first support and includes ticketing, collaboration and core automation. Freshdesk Omni adds a broader set of channels to a unified inbox. This choice affects plan price, setup steps and which channels appear in the workspace.</p>
<p>Open the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000011785-getting-started-with-freshdesk-omni">official onboarding guide</a> and compare the current channel and plan matrix. Do not assume a guide for Omni describes every option available in a standard Freshdesk account, especially if the account was created before December 2025.</p>
<h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Connect your support email</h2>
<p>Freshdesk can convert incoming support email into tickets. The current help article explains the supported email server options and the updated setup flow. Follow that guide for the exact controls shown in your account.</p>
<ol><li>Open the administration area and find the support email settings.</li><li>Add the address customers should use, or select the existing address you plan to connect.</li><li>Follow the instructions for the selected mail server and verify ownership where required.</li><li>Configure the forwarding or mailbox connection exactly as the guide describes.</li><li>Send a test message from an external mailbox and confirm that it appears as one ticket.</li><li>Reply from Freshdesk and check that the customer receives the reply and the conversation remains attached to the ticket.</li></ol>
<p>Keep the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37541-set-up-your-support-email">official support email setup guide</a> open during configuration. Do not copy generic mail server settings from an old tutorial because the product flow and supported options can change.</p>
<h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Organize agents, groups and ticket fields</h2>
<p>Set up ownership before you automate assignment. Create groups that reflect how the team handles work, not every small difference between customers. For example, a billing group may own invoice questions while a technical group handles troubleshooting. Decide who can move tickets between groups and who can change priority.</p>
<p>Use ticket fields only when they help someone make a decision, route a request or report on a useful outcome. Too many required fields slow down ticket creation and can lead agents to select random values. Start with a small set such as issue type, priority and product area, then add fields when the team has a clear use for them.</p>
<p>For shared queues, define what happens when a ticket is unassigned, when an agent is away and when a customer replies to a closed request. Write these rules down before adding automation. It is easier to debug one clear assignment policy than several rules that overlap.</p>
<h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Add ticket creation automation</h2>
<p>Freshdesk documents ticket creation automation rules that run when new tickets arrive. A rule has conditions and actions. For example, a rule can route a request to the billing group when the subject or description contains a billing term. The official article explains the current setup controls and how to test them.</p>
<ol><li>Open the automation area for ticket creation rules.</li><li>Create one rule for a clear use case, such as routing a billing request to the billing group.</li><li>Choose conditions that identify the request without matching unrelated tickets.</li><li>Choose the actions, such as setting a group, priority or field value.</li><li>Save the rule and test it with a new ticket that should match.</li><li>Test a second ticket that should not match, then adjust the conditions if it routes incorrectly.</li></ol>
<p>Start with a small set of rules and keep their names descriptive. Avoid creating two rules that set different groups for the same ticket unless you understand the order and intended outcome. Review the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37614-managing-tickets-using-the-dispatch-r">official ticket creation automation guide</a> before enabling a rule for the full queue.</p>
<h2 id="step-5">Step 5: Set service-level policies</h2>
<p>An SLA policy defines a target for the first response or resolution. Freshdesk lets administrators create policies for different ticket types and priorities. The exact controls depend on the account and product version, so use the current help article rather than assuming every older account has the same screen.</p>
<ol><li>Decide which priorities or request types need different targets.</li><li>Set business hours and holiday assumptions before assigning deadlines.</li><li>Define a first-response target that the team can meet during the stated support hours.</li><li>Define a resolution target only when the team has enough control over the work to make it useful.</li><li>Place the most specific policies before broad fallback rules when your configuration uses policy order.</li><li>Create sample tickets and confirm the target and timer behave as expected.</li></ol>
<p>The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37626">official SLA policy guide</a>, modified August 20, 2026, explains response and resolution targets and notes the current plan requirements. Check your plan before relying on multiple policies or advanced routing.</p>
<h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Test the customer journey</h2>
<p>Before sending customers to the new help desk, test a request from beginning to end. A ticket can be created successfully while its group, priority, SLA or notification is still wrong.</p>
<div class="software-detail__table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Expected result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Email receipt</th><td>One ticket appears with the correct requester and subject.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Assignment</th><td>The ticket reaches the intended group or agent.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Priority and SLA</th><td>The chosen priority and response target match the rule.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Agent reply</th><td>The customer receives a reply and the conversation remains in the ticket.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Customer portal</th><td>A customer can find the help content or submit a request through the published route.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Escalation</th><td>A high-priority or overdue ticket follows the expected path.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Keep a short list of the test ticket IDs and their expected outcomes. Fix failures before you invite the whole team. After launch, review the first few days of ticket assignment and response times instead of assuming the rules will remain correct as the queue changes.</p>
<h2 id="knowledge">Create a useful customer knowledge base</h2>
<p>Start with the questions agents answer repeatedly. A useful article explains one task, lists the required permissions and shows the expected result. Use titles customers would search for, such as resetting a password or checking an invoice, instead of internal department names.</p>
<ol><li>Group articles into a small number of customer-facing categories.</li><li>Write a direct opening sentence that answers the question.</li><li>Use numbered steps for tasks and a short troubleshooting section for common errors.</li><li>Check links and screenshots before publishing.</li><li>Ask an agent who did not write the article to follow it once.</li><li>Review search terms and ticket topics to decide what to improve next.</li></ol>
<p>Freshdesk plan materials list knowledge base and portal capabilities. Check the current plan matrix if a specific portal control or publication option is required. The knowledge base will only reduce ticket volume if its content is current and customers can find it.</p>
<h2 id="exports">Export records before a migration</h2>
<p>If Freshdesk is replacing another platform or your team needs a backup, use the official export workflow and confirm permissions first. Freshworks documents exports for tickets, contacts, companies and granular account data in CSV or Excel. The guide says an Admin or Supervisor role is required and export permissions must be enabled.</p>
<ol><li>Open the Tickets tab and choose the relevant ticket view, such as All tickets.</li><li>Apply filters for the time range, status, agent, group or type you need.</li><li>Choose Export and select the fields required for your audit or migration.</li><li>Repeat the export for other objects that matter, such as contacts or companies.</li><li>Open the files and check record counts, timestamps, custom fields and encoding.</li><li>Test how the destination platform handles comments, attachments, identities and custom fields before cancelling the source account.</li></ol>
<p>The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">official ticket export guide</a>, modified February 16, 2026, explains the current process. It does not guarantee that every object, comment, attachment or workflow will import into another product without changes. Treat the export as one step in a migration plan, not proof that the move is complete.</p>
<h2 id="troubleshooting">Common Freshdesk setup problems</h2>
<div class="software-detail__faq"><h3>Incoming email does not create a ticket</h3><p>Check the connected address, forwarding settings and verification status. Send a new message from an outside mailbox, then follow the current support email guide to review the mail connection. Do not reuse an old server configuration without checking it.</p></div>
<div class="software-detail__faq"><h3>A ticket reaches the wrong group</h3><p>Review ticket creation rules and the conditions that match the subject, description or fields. Disable overlapping rules temporarily in a test environment and confirm one rule at a time before changing production routing.</p></div>
<div class="software-detail__faq"><h3>An SLA target looks wrong</h3><p>Check priority, business hours, the ticket conditions and the policy that applies to the request. Confirm the plan supports the SLA behavior you configured and test a new ticket rather than relying only on an old ticket.</p></div>
<div class="software-detail__faq"><h3>An export is missing data</h3><p>Confirm your role and export permissions, the selected view and filters, and the fields included in the export. If a required object is missing, use the relevant account export option or ask Freshdesk support to confirm what is available.</p></div>
<h2>Keep the setup small and testable</h2>
<p>A clean Freshdesk rollout starts with a verified support channel and a small set of rules. Add more automation only when you can describe the condition, action and expected result. Review the <a href="/freshdesk/review/">Freshdesk review</a> for product fit, use the <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">pricing guide</a> to confirm plan limits, and compare <a href="/freshdesk/alternatives/">alternatives</a> if a required channel or workflow is not covered.</p>
HTML
